{title: When I Survey the Wondrous Cross}
{lyricist: Isaac Watts}
{composer: Edward Miller}
{year: 1707}
{meta: tune-year 1790}
{key: D}
{meta: tune ROCKINGHAM}
{meta: meter LM (8.8.8.8)}
{meta: variation The same text, sung to ROCKINGHAM}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: tags cross, communion, Lent}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
When [D]I sur[G]vey the [D]wondrous [A]cross
On [D]which the [Bm]Prince of [E7]glory [A]died,
My [D]richest [G]gain I [D]count but [Bm]loss,
And [G]pour con[D]tempt on [A]all my [D]pride.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
For[D]bid it, [G]Lord, that [D]I should [A]boast,
Save [D]in the [Bm]death of [E7]Christ my [A]God!
All [D]the vain [G]things that [D]charm me [Bm]most,
I [G]sacri[D]fice them [A]to His [D]blood.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
See, [D]from His [G]head, His [D]hands, His [A]feet,
Sor[D]row and [Bm]love flow [E7]mingled [A]down!
Did [D]e'er such [G]love and [D]sorrow [Bm]meet,
Or [G]thorns com[D]pose so [A]rich a [D]crown?
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Were [D]the whole [G]realm of [D]nature [A]mine,
That [D]were a [Bm]present [E7]far too [A]small;
Love [D]so a[G]mazing, [D]so di[Bm]vine,
De[G]mands my [D]soul, my [A]life, my [D]all.
{end_of_verse}
